import {REVISION131 as audit}from './revision131-data.mjs';
const n=(x,d=3)=>Number.isFinite(x)?x.toLocaleString('en-GB',{minimumFractionDigits:d,maximumFractionDigits:d}):'Unavailable';
const esc=s=>String(s).replace(/[&<>"']/g,c=>({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;',"'":'&#39;'}[c]));
export function revision131HTML(code){
 const s=audit.scoreCountries,c=audit.byCountry[code];
 return `<section class="panel content-panel" id="payroll-tax-evidence"><div class="eyebrow">CURRENT MODEL · ENGINE 1.31</div><h2>Tax adjustments follow the taxpayer</h2><p>The earlier budget bridge could return preliminary worker-tax withholding according to financial ownership. The model now returns that withholding to the workers who paid it. Extra worker-tax requests follow the same payroll recipients and respect each person's cash and remaining gross payroll. An unpaid request is reported; it is not shifted onto an unrelated household's assets.</p><p>Business income tax changes also now reach EU fiscal coverage: a legacy zero reference had made that control ineffective. Zero, lower and higher settings now change represented receipts, using the existing baseline control as the calibration reference. Opening empirical data, baseline tax targets and behavioural coefficients are unchanged. This corrects who pays or receives a represented wage-tax adjustment. It is not a national tax microsimulation or a refundable tax-credit programme. The source household-tax and social-contribution totals are broader than wages, so their allocation remains a proxy.</p><details><summary>Executed comparisons and remaining weaknesses</summary><p>${audit.paths} paths and ${n(audit.annualSteps,0)} annual steps cover all 30 presets, five seeds, long-run stress tests and retirement/tax interventions. All opening observations are preserved. Sector cash, saving less investment, current account and foreign assets, GDP and public/private debt continue to reconcile. Largest relative sector/finance residual: ${audit.maxResidual.toExponential(2)}.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Conditional retrospective diagnostic · 29 countries · percentage points</caption><thead><tr><th>Outcome</th><th>Earlier RMSE</th><th>Corrected RMSE</th><th>Earlier MAE</th><th>Corrected MAE</th></tr></thead><tbody>${[['Real-GDP growth · percentage points','gdp'],['Current account / GDP · percentage points','ca'],['Deficit / GDP · percentage points','deficit']].map(([label,key])=>`<tr><th scope="row">${label}</th><td>${n(s[key].earlier.RMSE)}</td><td>${n(s[key].candidate.RMSE)}</td><td>${n(s[key].earlier.MAE)}</td><td>${n(s[key].candidate.MAE)}</td></tr>`).join('')}</tbody></table></div><p>For ${esc(c.name)}, real-GDP growth RMSE changes from ${n(c.score.gdp.earlier.RMSE)} to ${n(c.score.gdp.candidate.RMSE)}. These revised 2024/25 observations were already examined, and the simulation uses later published fiscal inputs. This is not an untouched forecasting test; repeated seeds are not independent historical observations. EU27 is scored separately.</p><p>Correct payment recipients do not guarantee better GDP or inequality forecasts. Some errors worsen. The opening disposable-income Gini still proxies persistent gross earnings ranks; transfers and capital income can create an unrealistic later distribution. Century-long stress paths can become extreme. Those weaknesses remain visible. No coefficient was selected to force a GDP sign or improve this diagnostic.</p><p>Aggregate tax coverage still follows the lagged nominal-GDP budget base. Detailed pension taxation, progressivity, employer/employee tax separation and household survey records are not introduced without verified mappings. Uncollected worker-tax requests represent forgone cash revenue, not a separate modeled tax receivable.</p></details><p><a href="./data/revision131_validation.json" download>All paired results, errors and stress cases</a> · <a href="https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/taxing-wages-2025_b3a95829-en/full-report/methodology-and-limitations_49306c2c.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">OECD wage-tax methodology</a> · <a href="https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/revenue-statistics-2025_3a264267-en/full-report/disentangling-personal-income-tax-revenue-in-oecd-countries_d5d8741b.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Limits of the broad household-tax mapping</a></p></section>`;
}
